    "lawCode": "Amd §§16, 400 & 408, Ag & Mkts L; amd §752, add §753-f, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4234\n\nSPONSOR: GIANARIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the agriculture and markets law and\nthe general business law, in relation to the sale of dogs, cats and\nrabbits\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 48 of section 16 of the agriculture and\nmarkets law to provide training materials in accordance with the other\nsections of this act\n\nSection 2 amends subdivision 4 of section 400 of the agriculture and\nmarkets law, adding a new paragraph (d) to include retail pet shops as\ndefined in subdivision 8 of section 752 of the general business law\n\nSection 3 amends paragraphs (e) and (f) of subdivision 4 of section 408\nof the agriculture and markets law, and adds a new paragraph (g) to\nallow the department to deny the registration of a non-profit if the\nentity was previously licensed as a pet dealer\n\nSection 4 amends the opening paragraph of subdivision 3 of section 752\nof the general business law, and a new paragraph (d) is added to clarify\nthat retail pet shops are not considered pet dealers\n\nSection 5 amends section 752 of the general business law, adding a new\nsubdivision 8 to define \"retail pet shop\"\n\nSection 6 amends the general business law, adding a new section 753-f to\nprohibit the sale of dogs, cats and rabbits.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAt a time where there are healthy and loving pets waiting to be adopted\nfrom animal shelters statewide, there is simply no reason to allow the\nsale of puppy mill animals. Puppy mill animals are mistreated from\nbirth, and often develop behavioral or physical health problems later in\nlife despite their high price tags. This bill would encourage the\nadoption of dogs, cats and rabbits and ensure that animals no longer\nhave to face the cruelty, physical and psychological abuse to which the\npuppy mill supply chain subjects animals.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nOne hundred eightieth day after it shall have become law\n",
